    {
        "content": "<p>This revives an old concern of mine of using <code>Space d</code> for momentum / wave number vectors in the QM files. In classical mechanics this isn't an issue because it lives in the cotangent space, but so far we have been doing QM in flat space where the distinction isn't there. Do you have any thoughts on this, or even if it's worth addressing</p>",
        "id": 634003765,
        "sender_full_name": "Gregory Loges",
        "timestamp": 1792682797
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think it is likely worth addressing, since it will probably cause confusion in the long run.</p>",
        "id": 634204008,
        "sender_full_name": "Joseph Tooby-Smith",
        "timestamp": 1792728438
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Okay, I can start thinking about this! This is also an opportunity to introduce \"physicist\" conventions for Fourier transforms (we were tangling with factors of <code>2π</code> in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/1454\">#1454</a>).<br>\nI'll start a thread when I come up with a plan of attack / proposal.</p>",
        "id": 634392825,
        "sender_full_name": "Gregory Loges",
        "timestamp": 1792764427
    }
